==Finnish==
{{wikipedia|lang=fi}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*karva}}, an old {{der|fi|bat|-}} loan; derived from the same word from which e.g. {{cog|lt|gaũras}} is descended. Cognates include {{cog|vep|karv}}, {{cog|et|karv}} and {{cog|liv|kōra}}. Originally ''karva'' only had the meaning "color". In its current meaning it is first mentioned in a biblical text in 1621.<ref>{{R:Hakkinen 2004}}</ref> {{cln|fi|words derived through metathesis}}

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-pronunciation}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[hair]] {{gloss|single hair on human or animal}} {{q|not used of human head hair except in a technical sense}}
# [[fur]]
#: {{syn|fi|turkki}}
# {{lb|fi|figuratively}} [[hair]] {{gloss|small, tiny amount}}
# {{lb|fi|idiomatic}} [[colors]], [[stripes]]
#: {{uxi|fi|näyttää todellinen '''karva'''nsa|to [[show one's true colors|show one's true '''colors''']] / to [[show one's true stripes|show one's true '''stripes''']] {{gloss|to reveal how one really is}}}}
# {{lb|fi|dialectal|archaic}} [[color]]
#: {{syn|fi|väri}}

====Usage notes====
* A single hair of human head is {{m|fi|hius}}. A human head of hair is {{m|fi|tukka}} or {{m|fi||hiukset}}.

